{
 "code": "U01",
 "job": "First-year university student",
 "interviews": 243,
 "overview": [
  "Worry peaks in **AGI by 2028** (**3.39** of 5) and is lowest in **AGI by 2036** (**1.43**), with **AI Plateau** at 1.67.",
  "Their stakes are the **degree, the loan and family money**, which they mention even when AI barely changes anything.",
  "Most **learn tools, save and look for a second path**; **money cushion and adaptability** set how calm they stay, more than field or grades."
 ],
 "scenarios": {
  "S0": {
   "headline": "When AI mostly fizzles, first-years feel relieved about the degree but keep carrying the old worries of loans, rent and an average grade.",
   "whatTheyDo": [
    {
     "tag": "keep-going",
     "text": "About 89% change nothing, such as keeping the Saturday shift at the bike shop or the three early bakery shifts each week."
    },
    {
     "tag": "save-more",
     "text": "43% put money aside, for example 20 pounds a month into a savings account or staying at home through the whole degree."
    },
    {
     "tag": "learn-ai",
     "text": "35% keep using AI tools in a limited way, such as testing a simple quoting tool at Dad's shop."
    },
    {
     "tag": "side-income",
     "text": "34% look for extra work, like asking for more summer hours or tutoring two neighbours' children in maths on Saturdays."
    },
    {
     "tag": "change-job",
     "text": "21% look ahead to work that suits the degree, such as emailing three firms about placements before the careers fair."
    }
   ],
   "whatTheySay": [
    "They call it **hype**: \"every few years something is going to change everything, and it mostly doesn't.\"",
    "Many point to their own part-time job, where **tools still need a person** beside them.",
    "Online they would post little, because a quiet outcome **isn't news**."
   ],
   "whatTheyThink": [
    "Privately they feel **relieved**, but say nothing was solved: **loans, rent and thin graduate jobs** stay the same.",
    "Many worry more about **family money**, such as a parent's shop or a cut shift, than about AI.",
    "Some **don't trust tech firms** and expect little to change for ordinary workers."
   ],
   "howTheyFeel": [
    "Mostly **settled**: 107 of 243 feel calm and 110 uneasy, and nobody feels alarmed.",
    "The unease is **ordinary pressure** about money and grades, not fear of a new threat.",
    "A few feel a bit **smug or restless**, since they never changed their plans."
   ],
   "differences": [
    {
     "group": "Money cushion",
     "text": "Worry rises from 1.17 for first-years whose family pays all to 2.31 for those on loans and part-time work."
    },
    {
     "group": "Adaptability",
     "text": "Adaptive first-years worry less (1.42 against 1.83 for resistant ones), and 74% of them look for side income."
    },
    {
     "group": "Field of study",
     "text": "Business, law and humanities students save most (62% against 37% in health), while 98% of health students simply keep going."
    }
   ],
   "quotes": [
    {
     "id": "U01-11111",
     "name": "Aisha Abbott",
     "text": "I still owe loan money, and the graduate jobs were already thin before AI came along."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-21222",
     "name": "Yara Novak",
     "text": "If a tool gets my subject wrong, I'm not going to trust it on a subject I don't know."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-31223",
     "name": "Chloe Evans",
     "text": "I want to be there the moment a boy who can't say his 's' sounds says 'sun' for the first time."
    }
   ],
   "segments": {
    "least": {
     "name": "Students with family backing",
     "story": "None has a thin cushion and most are strong, so they keep going and often stay adaptive; family money, not AI news, gives them calm."
    },
    "most": {
     "name": "Loan-funded and stretched",
     "story": "Almost all have thin cushions and most resist change; their fear is about loans, shifts and family bills, not machines."
    }
   },
