  "S1": {
   "headline": "In a slow, steady AI rise, first-years worry that junior jobs thin out, so most learn the tools and look for ways to retrain.",
   "whatTheyDo": [
    {
     "tag": "learn-ai",
     "text": "81% plan to learn AI tools, such as trying one AI coding tool for a month to see what it does."
    },
    {
     "tag": "retrain",
     "text": "54% would add skills, such as asking lecturers which software jobs still need humans or learning the design software used in structures labs."
    },
    {
     "tag": "keep-going",
     "text": "31% mainly carry on, for example keeping the night job for steady money or practising hand calculations before using any software."
    },
    {
     "tag": "side-income",
     "text": "29% look for extra income, such as a second weekend job at a care home."
    },
    {
     "tag": "change-job",
     "text": "23% aim at placements, such as applying for a year in industry through the university scheme."
    }
   ],
   "whatTheySay": [
    "They say it's **real but no reason to panic**, and tell friends to learn what machines can't do.",
    "Many say **junior jobs go first**: \"junior coding jobs are the first ones that software will take.\"",
    "Others ask for **rules and paid retraining**, and some want unions in the room."
   ],
   "whatTheyThink": [
    "They fear firms won't fire anyone but will **quietly stop hiring juniors**, which is exactly their group.",
    "Many feel **behind on tools** they haven't touched, and worry that refusing them could cost a job.",
    "**Hands-on work** looks safer, but several admit they may be fooling themselves."
   ],
   "howTheyFeel": [
    "Mostly **uneasy to anxious**: 116 feel uneasy and 95 anxious, while 32 stay calm.",
    "Some feel **excited and nervous** together, as if a race has started.",
    "Many describe the **bottom rungs of the ladder** being sawn off, and fear being left behind."
   ],
   "differences": [
    {
     "group": "Adaptability",
     "text": "Adaptive first-years worry least (1.78 against 2.51 for resistant ones), and 74% of them add side income."
    },
    {
     "group": "Money cushion",
     "text": "Worry runs from 2.62 for those on thin cushions to 1.89 for those with strong ones."
    },
    {
     "group": "Outlook",
     "text": "53% of progressive first-years turn to collective action, while 41% of traditional ones reach for automation."
    }
   ],
   "quotes": [
    {
     "id": "U01-11111",
     "name": "Aisha Abbott",
     "text": "Why would a firm hire a junior when software does the checking?"
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-11233",
     "name": "Zane Bauer",
     "text": "I'd say stop reading headlines and build one thing this month. Fear is quiet when you're busy."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-23132",
     "name": "Esther Alvarez",
     "text": "The world changes but people still need each other. I'd say to learn the tools, work hard and stay kind."
    }
   ],
   "segments": {
    "least": {
     "name": "Adaptive and well backed",
     "story": "Most are adaptive with strong cushions, so they pick one thing to learn each month; practical action keeps their fear quiet."
    },
    "most": {
     "name": "Thin, slow to adapt",
     "story": "Half have thin cushions and most are resistant or steady; they fear no firm will hire a junior and they can't afford to retrain."
    }
   },
   "thoughts": [
