  "S2": {
   "headline": "If AI arrives by 2028, first-years fear their degree may be worth nothing and turn to family, savings and anyone who might help.",
   "whatTheyDo": [
    {
     "tag": "family-plans",
     "text": "53% talk to or plan with family, such as phoning Dad in the first month about stopping or changing degree."
    },
    {
     "tag": "save-more",
     "text": "51% save every spare pound, for example keeping the bakery shifts and putting aside every spare coin."
    },
    {
     "tag": "seek-support",
     "text": "51% look for advice, such as booking the university careers service or asking the faculty for a written plan on engineering jobs."
    },
    {
     "tag": "retrain",
     "text": "39% consider leaving for a trade, for example asking about an electrician apprenticeship and switching courses if one opens."
    },
    {
     "tag": "cut-spending",
     "text": "32% cut spending, such as paying only rent share, food and a bus pass."
    }
   ],
   "whatTheySay": [
    "They say **two years is nothing** and that governments are far behind and not ready.",
    "Many want **rules, a safety net and public support now**, some with taxes on firms or basic income trials.",
    "Others say families and communities **should look after each other** and not wait."
   ],
   "whatTheyThink": [
    "Nearly all think their **degree could be worthless** before they finish in 2029, with the loan still to pay.",
    "Many fear for **parents' jobs**, such as a bus driver, cleaner or pharmacist, and some feel guilty about family money.",
    "Several feel **no rules or retraining would be ready** in time."
   ],
   "howTheyFeel": [
    "**Anxious to alarmed**: 125 feel anxious and 106 alarmed, with nobody calm.",
    "Many describe a **stone in the chest**, a knot in the stomach or sleepless nights making lists.",
    "Some mix fear with **guilt** about disappointing parents who sacrificed for them."
   ],
   "differences": [
    {
     "group": "Adaptability",
     "text": "Resistant first-years worry most (3.64 against 3.01 for adaptive ones); 68% of resistant ones seek support and 70% of adaptive ones look for side income."
    },
    {
     "group": "Money cushion",
     "text": "Thin cushions score 3.58 against 3.22 for strong ones; 70% of strong-cushion students make family plans."
    },
    {
     "group": "Outlook",
     "text": "65% of progressive first-years turn to politics, while 68% of traditional ones seek support."
    }
   ],
   "quotes": [
    {
     "id": "U01-11111",
     "name": "Aisha Abbott",
     "text": "My mum's bus job would last longer than my career, which is a strange thing to say."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-11332",
     "name": "Umar Ziegler",
     "text": "A community that looks after its own doesn't have to wait for a handout."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-23131",
     "name": "Wei Tanaka",
     "text": "If I knew exactly how my parents' restaurant is doing, I could plan properly and help."
    }
   ],
   "segments": {
    "least": {
     "name": "Backed and building alternatives",
     "story": "Most have strong cushions and are adaptive, so they use the two years to build a second path or rely on their community."
    },
    "most": {
     "name": "Alarmed about parents too",
     "story": "Most are resistant or steady and many have thin cushions; they fear for the degree and for a parent's job at once."
    }
