  "S4": {
   "headline": "With ten years, first-years feel mostly calm: it's a long time to qualify, save and build skills, though many doubt anyone will act early.",
   "whatTheyDo": [
    {
     "tag": "save-more",
     "text": "67% save, for example starting a pension from the first graduate paycheck or saving toward a deposit for a small flat."
    },
    {
     "tag": "keep-going",
     "text": "51% finish the degree and carry on, such as applying for graduate jobs in year three."
    },
    {
     "tag": "family-plans",
     "text": "47% plan with family, for example helping Mum and Dad with bills as they age."
    },
    {
     "tag": "learn-ai",
     "text": "43% learn the tools slowly, such as trying one AI tool on small coursework tasks."
    },
    {
     "tag": "retrain",
     "text": "37% add a skill over time, such as an evening class in basic electrics or one new skill each year."
    }
   ],
   "whatTheySay": [
    "They say **ten years is plenty of time** if people use it, and tell friends to build skills and savings.",
    "Many say **don't panic and don't ignore it**, and tell friends to keep learning.",
    "Some want the decade used for **fair rules, apprenticeships and unions**."
   ],
   "whatTheyThink": [
    "Many feel **survivable**: at 28 they'd have years of work, savings and some loan paid off.",
    "They doubt that others will act early, and expect people to **put it off until it's urgent**.",
    "Some still worry about **parents' pensions** and about friends in office jobs."
   ],
   "howTheyFeel": [
    "Mostly **calm**: 147 of 243 are calm, 87 uneasy and only 9 anxious.",
    "Many call ten years a **runway** or a normal career challenge.",
    "A background **hum of unease** stays, about parents and whether the decade is wasted."
   ],
   "differences": [
    {
     "group": "Adaptability",
     "text": "Adaptive first-years worry least (1.06 against 1.75 for resistant ones), and 63% look for side income while 57% invest."
    },
    {
     "group": "Money cushion",
     "text": "Worry is 1.63 with a thin cushion and 1.27 with a strong one; 77% of the thin group save more."
    },
    {
     "group": "Outlook",
     "text": "69% of traditional first-years make family plans, against 47% overall."
    }
   ],
   "quotes": [
    {
     "id": "U01-11113",
     "name": "Iris Moreau",
     "text": "With ten, I can fail at a business or two and still be fine. That's the biggest difference."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-11211",
     "name": "Diego Dubois",
     "text": "If I'm panicked I refuse out of fear, but if I've got time I get curious."
    },
    {
     "id": "U01-31222",
     "name": "Umar Yilmaz",
     "text": "Time lets me do the right thing, not just the quick thing."
    }
   ],
   "segments": {
    "least": {
     "name": "Adaptive, time to try",
     "story": "Half are adaptive and most have moderate or strong cushions, so they see ten years as room to try, fail and build."
    },
    "most": {
     "name": "Resistant, thin cushions",
     "story": "Almost all are resistant and most have thin cushions; with less room for error, even a decade feels shaky until they get started."
    }
   },
